{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1082", "sample_idx": 21, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Liam jolted awake to the sound of pixelated static, his fingers pressed into the cold, synthetic surface beneath him—until he flexed and realized it was grass, real but wrong. The sky stretched too evenly overhead, a perfect, unblemished blue, and when he blinked, his vision flickered at the edges like a buffering screen. His watch, usually a sleek titanium model, now displayed jagged, green text: *\"NPC HUD: LOW (78%).\"* A nearby squirrel froze mid-sprint, its tail pixelating before it bolted upright, eyes glowing red for a split second. Liam’s stomach dropped as he noticed his reflection in a puddle: his edges weren’t quite smooth, and his shadow stretched too long, flickering like a corrupted sprite. He reached for his phone—dead. Then he saw the minimap in the corner of his vision, pulsing with his location and an ominous red circle labeled *\"Boss: The Watcher (200m).\"*\n\nHe stumbled through the woods, his footsteps leaving behind a faint trail of blue sparks, like deleted data reforming. Villagers in the distance moved in stiff, repetitive loops, their dialogue glitching into loops of *\"Welcome... welcome... welcome...\"* when he got too close. A child ran toward him, but her face dissolved into static mid-stride, replaced by a loading screen—*\"PLAYER LOAD FAIL.\"* Liam’s hands trembled as he found a crumbling \"Game Master\" signpost, its letters half-rendered: *\"If you see this, CONGRATULATIONS. You’re stuck. Find the Glitch Core before The Watcher does.\"* Behind him, the trees twisted into a corridor of distorted geometry, the air humming with a low, familiar tune—the same one that played when his old arcade game overloaded. He clenched his fists. Somewhere in this loop, there had to be a way out. And right now, *he* was the only one who could press *reset*.", "logprobs": [{"token": "L", "logprob": -1.2783106565475464}, {"to